//? Title: Floyd's tortoise and hare: actual meetings, entry recovery and work
//? Subtitle: Preserve the original rho example and seven modular workloads; inspect all three native phases
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Iterating a deterministic function on a finite state space eventually enters a cycle.
Floyd's detector finds the least tail length and period using a constant number of
state variables. Its slow and fast pointers first meet, then recover the entry and
measure the period. This report preserves the original seven-state rho graph and
seven quadratic modular workloads, records actual pointer pairs from the native
kernel, and distinguishes completed transition counts from runtime or random-map
claims. Checked budgets and owned traces expose the practical API contract.
)

\toc

> The orbit, not the whole transition graph

Write x_i=f^i(x_0). Determinism makes every state after the first repeated value
repeat in the same order. The least entry index mu is nonnegative; the least period
lambda is positive. The orbit has mu distinct tail states followed by lambda distinct
cycle states. The answer depends on x_0, even when the complete transition function
contains other disconnected cycles.

The name tortoise and hare refers to the detection pair x_t and x_(2t). This is
cycle finding for a function, not the all-pairs shortest-path algorithm also named
for Floyd. Brent's 1980 paper explicitly describes the detector as attributed to Floyd
by Knuth: [author-hosted paper](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/brent/pd/rpb051i.pdf).

The first program preserves the original successor table [1,2,3,4,5,6,3], now obtained
from the native rho fixture. The seven-state diagram draws that table directly.
The ordinary Floyd and Brent results agree on tail length three and period four.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/floyd_graph.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto next=dm::algorithms::rho_successors(3,4);const dm::algorithms::CycleFunction f=[&](auto x){return next.at(x);};
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::floyd_cycle_work(0,f),b=dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_work(0,f);
  std::print("Original rho: Floyd mu {}; lambda {}; entry {}; evaluations {}.\n",a.result.mu,a.result.lambda,a.entry,a.evaluations);
  std::print("Original rho: Brent mu {}; lambda {}; entry {}; evaluations {}.\n",b.result.mu,b.result.lambda,b.entry,b.evaluations);
  std::print("Native orbit prefix: {}.\n",dm::algorithms::iterate_states(0,f,11));
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> positions{{-3,0},{0,0},{3,0},{6,0},{9,0},{9,-3},{6,-3}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=1.7;style.height=1.3;style.font_size=26;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<next.size();++i){auto node_style=style;if(i==a.entry)node_style.fill={191,219,254};d.node(std::to_string(i),positions[i],std::to_string(i),node_style);}
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<next.size();++i)d.edge(std::to_string(i),std::to_string(next[i]));
  d.text({0,1.8},"Tail: three states",25).text({7.5,-5},"Cycle: four states; entry 3",25).save_svg("build/floyd_graph.svg");
  if(a.result.mu!=3||a.result.lambda!=4||b.result.mu!=3||b.result.lambda!=4)throw std::runtime_error("original rho");
}
