\caption(The seven original mu-plus-lambda values, whole-operation evaluation counts and rounded ratios are preserved. The horizontal positions identify workloads rather than imply a fitted growth curve.)
\alttext(Two native point plots show Floyd and Brent total call counts and their ratios for the seven original moduli; each modulus is a labeled categorical position.)

These seven measured ratios span 1.12 to 1.59. They are deterministic work observations,
not timings, a universal ratio interval, or proof of square-root growth with modulus.
A random-function model is an additional assumption; a quadratic map selected once
per modulus does not sample that model. The modulus alone does not determine the
orbit's tail and period.

The polynomial is inspired by the iteration in
[Pollard's 1975 paper](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/afs/cs/academic/class/15451-f11/www/lectures/lect1122_Pollard.pdf),
which illustrates a minus-one polynomial and uses additional difference products and
gcd tests to find factors. Our plus-one transition and exact-state cycle detectors do
not perform that factorization procedure. Their full-state cycle lengths cannot be
read as the number of calls needed to discover a prime factor.

> Change the tail while holding the period fixed

Controlled rho fixtures isolate geometry from the choice of modulus. With period
eight, the first meeting t jumps to the next positive multiple of eight as the tail
crosses that boundary. Entry recovery adds two calls per extra tail state. The next
program measures those stair steps directly; the curve is joined only because these
are consecutive controlled integer inputs, not a fitted probabilistic trend.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/floyd_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> mu,fc,bc,meeting;
  std::print("| tail mu | period | first meeting t | Floyd total | Brent total |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t tail=0;tail<=32;++tail){const auto next=dm::algorithms::rho_successors(tail,8);const dm::algorithms::CycleFunction f=[&](auto value){return next.at(value);};const auto a=dm::algorithms::floyd_cycle_work(0,f),b=dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_work(0,f);
    mu.push_back(double(tail));fc.push_back(double(a.evaluations));bc.push_back(double(b.evaluations));meeting.push_back(double(a.detection_evaluations/3));if(tail%8==0||tail%8==1)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",tail,a.result.lambda,a.detection_evaluations/3,a.evaluations,b.evaluations);
  }
  auto total=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();total.lines(mu,fc,"Floyd",{220,38,38},2).lines(mu,bc,"Brent",{37,99,235},2).title("Whole-operation callback work").xlab("Tail length mu; period fixed at 8").ylab("Completed f evaluations").legend({"Floyd","Brent"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}});
  auto collision=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();collision.points(mu,meeting,"Meeting",{22,163,74},3).title("First positive meeting round").xlab("Tail length mu; period fixed at 8").ylab("t with x_t = x_(2t)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(total).add(collision).size(1100,540).title("Controlled orbit geometry exposes the count changes").fit_panels().save_svg("build/floyd_work.svg");
}
