\image(figures/floyd_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A successful trace owns twelve pairs from twenty-two calls. Either demonstrated limit stops after twenty-one calls; neither failure returns a partial trace or undoes callback effects.)
\alttext(Three native boxes compare the successful twenty-two-call twelve-pair trace with evaluation-limit and row-cap failures after twenty-one completed calls.)

The detectors borrow a nonempty deterministic uint64 callback only for the invocation.
Callback exceptions propagate, and completed external effects are not undone.
Owned records contain integer states and counters, so they survive destruction of a
fixture table or callback capture. Empty callbacks reject before movement. Arithmetic
counters and indices throw on overflow. Without a practical budget, even a finite
state orbit can require an impractically long traversal.

`quadratic_cycle_step` accepts all uint64 states and constants, rejects modulus zero,
and returns zero for modulus one. Widening before the square matters: a wrapped
uint64 polynomial followed by a remainder can define a different map. That helper
is reusable independently of either detector.

All five complete editable programs call the canonical C++ APIs. Native figures are
saved locally and copied into the report; no external dataset is required. Verification
preserves original callback inputs and outputs, results and phase counts, compares
exhaustive small maps with an independent first-repeat oracle, checks actual trace
indices and call order, tests every budget boundary on representative orbits, and
checks full-width polynomial residues with independent binary modular arithmetic.
